se thee:¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙZfor oftentimes also thine own heart knoweth that thou thyself likewise hast cursed others.¦layout ªlast_verse…¤book§chapter«first_verseªlast_verse¦verses“„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙQAll this have I proved by wisdom: I said, I will be wise; but it was far from me.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ?That which is far off, and exceeding deep, who can find it out?¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ¤I applied mine heart to know, and to search, and to seek out wisdom, and the reason of things, and to know the wickedness of folly, even of foolishness and madness:¦layout ªlast_verse…¤book§chapter«first_verseªlast_verse¦verses‘„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ´and I find more bitter than death the woman, whose heart is snares and nets, and her hands as bands: whoso pleaseth God shall escape from her; but the sinner shall be taken by her.¦layout ªlast_verse…¤book§chapter«first_verseªlast_verse¦verses“„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ\Behold, this have I found, saith the Preacher, counting one by one, to find out the account:¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙwhich yet my soul seeketh, but I find not: one man among a thousand have I found; but a woman among all those have I not found.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙeLo, this only have I found, that God hath made man upright; but they have sought out many inventions.¦layout ªlast_verse